    "summary": "High‑octane first set and smoking \"The Other One\" jam",
    "blurb": "Features a blistering first set, a marathon The Other One in the second set, and an encore of Ramble On Rose. Jerry stays on a roll with bluegrass‑style picking, Phil drives the jams, and Keith’s piano adds texture despite modest audio quality.",
    "review": "The November 17, 1971 show at Albuquerque Civic Auditorium finds the Dead firing on all cylinders. From the opening Truckin' through a rollicking run of classics like Sugaree, Tennessee Jed, and Jack Straw, the first set maintains a high‑octane groove that never lets up. The real highlight erupts in the second set where a smoking \"The Other One\" sequence—spanning two full stretches with interludes of Me And My Uncle—showcases Phil’s bass pushing riffs while Jerry’s guitar rides a relentless wave of improvisation. Keith’s piano weaves beautiful accents throughout, and Bill Kreutzmann is an animal on the kit, propelling the jams forward. The night caps off with a heartfelt Ramble On Rose encore that leaves the audience buzzing. Though captured from a mono FM broadcast microphone with occasional boomy bass and tape splices, the recording remains listenable enough to convey the band’s inspired playing.",
